well the verdict's still being read out  in the court behind me but at nine  minutes past ten local the judge said  the defendant imguard f is found guilty  of aiding the murder of more than 10 000  people and five cases of attempted  murder in stuttoff concentration camp  just to give you some background to this  case she is a 97 year old who when she  was 18 worked at stuchhof concentration  camp which is near Gdansk in Poland she  worked as a secretary for the SS  commander and She's accused of basically  aiding the killing of more than 10 000  people in in the fact that she had  according to the prosecution knowledge  of what was going on in that camp and to  give you an idea of what was going on in  that camp more than 65 000 people died  uh Institute of concentration camp some  were gassed shot starved worked to death  it was incredibly cruel place in Winter  they were left with very little clothes  very little food made to work very long  hours a few hours sleep one of the  former people who were held in there  told us that at night people often died  in their beds to the point that every  day they had to check all of the bunk  beds and bring the bodies to the  crematorium and that the fires the  furnaces that burned the bodies went 24  hours a day but there were so many  bodies that they just couldn't keep up  with the demand so imguard ferchner was  accused of knowing about this she said  really very little throughout this trial  at the end  of the hearings she said that she was  sorry about what had happened in  stuttoff Camp but really denied any  responsibility for what was going on she  claimed that she didn't know that is  something that survivors have challenged  we went to strohoff to see where she  worked and her office actually overlooks  the camp and they were questioning how  could she not know what was happening to  the thousands of people that were being  kept there how could she not smell the  smoke from the fires at the bodies were  being burned how could she not see the  rows of prisoners starved and frightened  walking in front of her window every  morning now the judge has made this  verdict but it's an important case  because this is likely to be one of the  last cases that we hear in these Nazi  trials because over the last few years  Germany has been trying to bring former  Nazis to justice but because of their  ages and as I said imguard is 97 many of  them have become too old so she is  likely to be the last case which is  tried in these Nazi trials
 

